Earth Engine into Google Cloud,

00:38:50.530 --> 00:38:53.860
and that integration is
now in private preview.

00:38:53.860 --> 00:38:55.780
Today I'll demo a
workflow that combines

00:38:55.780 --> 00:38:58.510
Vertex AI, Earth Engine.

00:38:58.510 --> 00:39:01.480
BigQuery, and
Google Maps platform

00:39:01.480 --> 00:39:03.880
to show how Google Cloud makes
it incredibly easy for you

00:39:03.880 --> 00:39:07.670
to innovate and deliver
insights and do it quickly.

00:39:07.670 --> 00:39:09.220
So here's a scenario.

00:39:09.220 --> 00:39:10.660
You work at an
insurance company,

00:39:10.660 --> 00:39:12.618
and you need to analyze
your company's exposure

00:39:12.618 --> 00:39:13.545
to flood risk.

00:39:13.545 --> 00:39:15.670
You think New buildings
may be a strong contributor

00:39:15.670 --> 00:39:19.210
to that risk, and you want
to test your hypothesis.

00:39:19.210 --> 00:39:22.630
To do that, we first need to
understand where the built

00:39:22.630 --> 00:39:24.580
environment is expanding.

00:39:24.580 --> 00:39:27.010
In other words, we just need
to categorize the surface

00:39:27.010 --> 00:39:29.050
of the entire planet.

00:39:29.050 --> 00:39:31.930
That could be hard, but
Vertex AI offers the tooling

00:39:31.930 --> 00:39:34.510
to develop a best
in class ML model,

00:39:34.510 --> 00:39:38.640
and Earth Engine provides
constantly updated data.

00:39:38.640 --> 00:39:39.930
Let's fast forward a bit.

00:39:39.930 --> 00:39:42.900
We finished training our
model, and now Earth Engine

00:39:42.900 --> 00:39:45.180
is sending satellite
imagery to be categorized,

00:39:45.180 --> 00:39:46.890
so your understanding
of the world

00:39:46.890 --> 00:39:49.890
can update in near real time.

00:39:49.890 --> 00:39:51.740
Here's the Earth
Engine script showing

00:39:51.740 --> 00:39:53.000
the results of that model.

00:39:53.000 --> 00:39:55.850
This area in red is
where the model estimates

00:39:55.850 --> 00:39:57.260
the locations of buildings.

00:39:57.260 --> 00:40:00.260
That's your current
built environment.

00:40:00.260 --> 00:40:04.950
To find the change over time, we
need a few more lines of code.

00:40:04.950 --> 00:40:09.600
These lines of code give us
a built environment in 2016.

00:40:09.600 --> 00:40:11.940
And here, we calculate
the difference

00:40:11.940 --> 00:40:14.520
between 2016 and today.

00:40:14.520 --> 00:40:18.480
When there's a change, it
shows up in purple on the map.

00:40:18.480 --> 00:40:21.640
This is where there
are new buildings.

00:40:21.640 --> 00:40:24.340
So next we're going to
export sample and export

00:40:24.340 --> 00:40:26.815
the data so we can do
additional analysis in BigQuery.

00:40:29.580 --> 00:40:31.590
Over in the BigQuery
console, this script

00:40:31.590 --> 00:40:35.400
clusters those data points
here and then outputs

00:40:35.400 --> 00:40:38.220
polygons that show the areas
with the biggest changes

00:40:38.220 --> 00:40:41.040
in the built environment.

00:40:41.040 --> 00:40:42.750
So at this point, you
have a few options.

00:40:42.750 --> 00:40:44.960
You could combine this
data with flood locations

00:40:44.960 --> 00:40:48.530
you identify around the
world also with Earth Engine.

00:40:48.530 --> 00:40:50.630
Maybe you want to enhance
your model with weather

00:40:50.630 --> 00:40:52.520
data and physical terrain data.

00:40:52.520 --> 00:40:54.920
That's available in
Earth Engine too.

00:40:54.920 --> 00:40:57.380
You could also include data
on your company's insurance

00:40:57.380 --> 00:41:02.120
portfolio to gain additional
insight into critical risks.

00:41:02.120 --> 00:41:04.310
We'll wrap up this
demo by visualizing

00:41:04.310 --> 00:41:08.060
our results and a new feature
available on Google Maps

00:41:08.060 --> 00:41:12.260
platform, the open source
data viz library deck.gl

00:41:12.260 --> 00:41:14.780
with a BigQuery connector
provided by [INAUDIBLE]..

00:41:14.780 --> 00:41:17.810
So we now have a clear picture
of where the built environment

00:41:17.810 --> 00:41:21.080
is changing and where to
focus next for our work

00:41:21.080 --> 00:41:22.790
on flood risks.

00:41:22.790 --> 00:41:26.060
In summary, no
wrangling data, no need

00:41:26.060 --> 00:41:29.220
